Java Code:
// Main interface
interface FileHandler {
    void open();
    void close();
}
// Tagging interface
interface Secure {}
// PDF class
class PDFFile implements FileHandler, Secure {
    public void open() {
        System.out.println("Opening PDF file");
    }
    public void close() {
        System.out.println("Closing PDF file");
    }
}
// Word class
class WordFile implements FileHandler {
    public void open() {
        System.out.println("Opening Word file");
    }
    public void close() {
        System.out.println("Closing Word file");
    }
}
Explanation:
FileHandler is an interface that defines the abstraction for common file operations (open() and close()). Secure is a tagging interface�it has no methods but acts as a marker. PDFFile implements both interfaces. We can later use instanceof checks to identify secure classes:
if (file instanceof Secure) {
    System.out.println("Security checks enabled.");
}
Benefits:
Abstraction: Interfaces let you define what operations must be implemented, not how.
Multiple inheritance: Java allows classes to implement multiple interfaces.
Tagging: Marker interfaces like Secure enable metadata tagging without altering logic.

